 ('frz3', 'Megasthenes describes Pataliputra as a great city on the river, defended by a timber palisade with hundreds of towers and many gates — and excavation has in fact found massive wooden palisade remains, which is a satisfying case of a text and the ground agreeing.'),
 ('frz4', 'He describes its administration by committees handling crafts, foreigners, births and deaths, trade, manufactures and tax collection. How literally to take his numbers is debated; the structure is plausible, the detail is secondhand.'),
 ('frz5', 'And the money. Land revenue was the backbone, a share of the produce, assessed and collected by a salaried bureaucracy rather than farmed out. The state ran mines, forests and workshops directly.'),
 ('frz6', 'It invested in irrigation: the Sudarshana lake in Saurashtra was built under the Mauryas, and we know that because a later inscription records repairing it. A standing army and a large officialdom were paid out of this, which is exactly what the Nandas had shown was possible, on a far bigger scale.'),
 ('frz7', "After Ashoka's death about 232 BCE, the empire shrinks quickly. The later Mauryas are barely known."),
