 ('plq1', 'Why did it fall? The explanations divide. One group of arguments is economic and administrative: an empire this centralised needed constant revenue and able kings, and had neither for long; the provinces had no independent strength to fall back on. Another is political: weak successors, revolts, and pressure from the north-west.'),
 ('plq2', "An older view blamed Ashoka's pacifism and his patronage of Buddhism for weakening the state — that one is now largely rejected, because the empire outlasted him by half a century and the edicts show no dismantling of the army. Say which explanation you prefer and why."),
 ('ins1', 'Here is the memory tip, carved in stone. Taxila, Ujjain, Suvarnagiri, Tosali — four provinces, one capital at Pataliputra.'),
 ('quiz1', 'Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. First: the officers the edicts give authority over large rural areas. The rajukas.'),
 ('quiz2', "Second: the irrigation work in Saurashtra built under the Mauryas and repaired by later rulers. The Sudarshana lake. Third: the Greek ambassador's account of India, which survives only as fragments quoted by later writers."),
 ('quiz3', 'The Indica. In Mains, a frequent question asks why the Mauryan empire declined.'),
 ('quiz4', 'Set out the administrative, economic and political explanations, say which you find strongest, and explain why the pacifism argument is no longer accepted.'),
